      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let me provide you with the facts.\n\nIf you've underdeclared foreign assets in your UK estate planning, you may face penalties from HMRC. The penalties for failure to notify HMRC about foreign assets can be substantial. The 'failure to notify' penalty is £3,000 per year, but this can increase to £9,000 per year if the underpaid tax is £25,000 or more.\n\nAdditionally, if you've intentionally tried to evade IHT, you may face more severe penalties, including the 'intentional failure to notify' penalty, which can be up to £50,000 per year. You may also face interest on the underpaid tax, which can be significant.\n\nIt's essential to note that HMRC has a 'voluntary disclosure' scheme, which allows you to come forward and disclose previously undisclosed foreign assets. This can help you avoid penalties, but it's crucial to do so before HMRC discovers the assets themselves.\n\nRegarding the 'gift' aspect, if you've gifted assets to someone in a foreign country without declaring them, you may face IHT on the gift, even if the recipient is not a UK resident. This is",
